From e7eaa8f8c69b490f72b080f4974f891aefc48924 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rkvenc forward-port <forward-port@local>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 22:59:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] video: rockchip: rga: don't drop mm->lock during
 session-release teardown

rga_mm_session_release_buffer() iterates mm->memory_idr under mm->lock and,
for the exiting session's buffers, called
kref_put(&buffer->refcount, rga_mm_kref_release_buffer). On the last
reference that release callback does mutex_unlock(&mm->lock) around the
sleeping rga_mm_unmap_buffer() and then mutex_lock(&mm->lock) again -- i.e.
it drops and re-acquires mm->lock in the middle of idr_for_each_entry()
during teardown.

Under heavy session churn this left rga_drvdata->mm->lock owned by a task
that had already exited and had its task_struct RCU-freed. The next waiter
on mm->lock -- e.g. the rkrga/mm_session debugfs reader -- then entered the
mutex adaptive-spin path (mutex_can_spin_on_owner reads the freed owner
task_struct), which KASAN flagged as a slab-use-after-free, and blocked
forever on the permanently-held lock in an unkillable D state (reboot to
recover).

Free the last reference in place with rga_mm_force_releaser_buffer(), which
unmaps and frees while continuing to hold mm->lock (as the BSP does), so no
lock is dropped mid-iteration. The refcount>1 (shared-buffer) path already
only decrements, so its kref_put() never runs the release callback and is
left as-is.

Also NULL-guard dump_buffer->session in rga_mm_session_show(): the same
teardown path sets buffer->session = NULL for still-shared buffers, so the
debugfs dump must not assume a live session.

Regression from bc086cbe03d72c ("rkvenc forward-port" RGA lifetime rework);
not present in the Rockchip BSP.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_debugger.c |  8 +++++++-
 drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_mm.c       | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 26 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_debugger.c b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_debugger.c
index 59d6464cfff7c..3a9b1b69da5ba 100644
--- a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_debugger.c
+++ b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_debugger.c
@@ -271,9 +271,15 @@ static int rga_mm_session_show(struct seq_file *m, void *data)
 	seq_puts(m, "===============================================================\n");
 
 	idr_for_each_entry(&mm_session->memory_idr, dump_buffer, id) {
+		/*
+		 * rga_mm_session_release_buffer() detaches a still-referenced
+		 * buffer from its exiting owner by setting session = NULL, so
+		 * this dump must not assume a live session.
+		 */
 		seq_printf(m, "handle = %d refcount = %d mm_flag = 0x%x	tgid = %d\n",
 			   dump_buffer->handle, kref_read(&dump_buffer->refcount),
-			   dump_buffer->mm_flag, dump_buffer->session->tgid);
+			   dump_buffer->mm_flag,
+			   dump_buffer->session ? dump_buffer->session->tgid : -1);
 
 		switch (dump_buffer->type) {
 		case RGA_DMA_BUFFER:
diff --git a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_mm.c b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_mm.c
index 4be9f18a2ae55..131c349947932 100644
--- a/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_mm.c
+++ b/drivers/video/rockchip/rga3/rga_mm.c
@@ -3188,12 +3188,29 @@ int rga_mm_session_release_buffer(struct rga_session *session)
 			 * for the owner here.
 			 */
 			buffer->session = NULL;
+			/*
+			 * refcount > 1, so this only decrements: it never runs
+			 * rga_mm_kref_release_buffer() and therefore never drops
+			 * mm->lock -- safe to call inside idr_for_each_entry().
+			 */
+			kref_put(&buffer->refcount, rga_mm_kref_release_buffer);
 		} else {
 			rga_err("[tgid:%d] Destroy handle[%d] when the user exits\n",
 			       session->tgid, buffer->handle);
+			/*
+			 * Last reference: free it while still holding mm->lock.
+			 * Must NOT use kref_put(rga_mm_kref_release_buffer) here --
+			 * that release callback drops and re-acquires mm->lock
+			 * around the sleeping unmap, i.e. mid-idr_for_each_entry().
+			 * On heavy session teardown that left mm->lock owned by an
+			 * exited/freed task and wedged the next mm->lock waiter
+			 * (e.g. rga_mm_session_show) in an unkillable D state with a
+			 * KASAN use-after-free on the freed task_struct. Freeing in
+			 * place under the held lock (as the BSP does) is
+			 * iteration-safe and drops no lock.
+			 */
+			rga_mm_force_releaser_buffer(buffer);
 		}
-
-		kref_put(&buffer->refcount, rga_mm_kref_release_buffer);
 	}
 
 	mutex_unlock(&mm->lock);
